menu "Capstan"

comment "Target board"

choice CAPSTAN_BOARD
    prompt "Rotary display board"
    default CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    help
      Selects the panel, touch controller and pin map compiled into
      components/capstan_board, and the generated UI variant compiled from
      main/ui<res>/.

      All three boards carry the same ESP32-S3R8 (8 MB octal PSRAM, 16 MB
      flash, native USB-Serial/JTAG), so nothing outside the board component
      and the UI variant changes between them.

config CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    bool "Makerfabs MaTouch ESP32-S3 Rotary 2.1in (480x480, ST7701S RGB)"
    help
      2.1" round IPS, 480x480, ST7701S over a 16-bit RGB565 parallel bus
      with an SPI sideband for the init sequence. CST8266 capacitive touch.
      Needs PSRAM framebuffers and internal-RAM bounce buffers.

config CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    bool "Elecrow CrowPanel 1.46in Rotary (360x360, JD9855)"
    help
      1.46" round IPS, 360x360, JD9855.

config CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    bool "Elecrow CrowPanel 1.28in Rotary (240x240, GC9A01 SPI)"
    help
      1.28" round IPS, 240x240, GC9A01 over SPI. The smallest panel: its UI
      variant uses a compact keyboard and a smaller type scale, because the
      480x480 layouts do not fit. The app menu is the SAME carousel as the
      other two boards -- it shows three items whatever the list length, so
      it needs no more room here than anywhere else.

endchoice

config CAPSTAN_LCD_H_RES
    int
    default 480 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default 360 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default 240 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128

config CAPSTAN_LCD_V_RES
    int
    default 480 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default 360 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default 240 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128

config CAPSTAN_BOARD_NAME
    string
    default "matouch21" if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default "crowpanel146" if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default "crowpanel128" if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128

config CAPSTAN_LCD_BGR
    bool "Panel expects BGR element order"
    depends on !CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default y if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    default n
    help
      Swaps red and blue on the SPI panels. If the red swatch renders blue
      and blue renders red while green is correct, this is the control.

      VERIFIED on hardware, per board, by looking at the panel:

        CrowPanel 1.28in (GC9A01)  -- y (BGR)
        CrowPanel 1.46in (JD9855)  -- n (RGB)

      The two boards genuinely need OPPOSITE values. That is not a bug in
      the mapping and it is not worth "fixing" into a single default: they
      are different controllers driving differently-bonded glass, and the
      only authority on which one a given panel wants is the panel.

      Do not try to derive this from the vendor's LovyanGFX config either.
      Its `cfg.rgb_order` does not map onto esp_lcd's element order the way
      the names suggest -- the 1.28in has rgb_order=false and the 1.46in
      has rgb_order=true, which is the exact inverse of what each board
      actually needs here.

      History, because this one hid for a long time: an earlier version
      derived the element order from `rgb_order` directly, giving the
      1.28in RGB and the 1.46in BGR -- wrong on BOTH boards. It was caught
      on the 1.46in first, and the 1.28in was then recorded as "verified
      correct" purely because nobody had yet held a known-red swatch up to
      it. Colour correctness is not something a screen full of UI chrome
      will tell you. Check it against a deliberate red/green/blue test
      pattern or do not claim it.

      The MaTouch is excluded: it is an RGB parallel panel whose channel
      order is set by the data pin grouping, via CAPSTAN_LCD_SWAP_RB.

config CAPSTAN_LCD_MIRROR_X
    bool "Mirror the display horizontally"
    default y if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    default n
    help
      Flips the panel left-to-right. If text reads backwards -- mirrored
      rather than upside down -- this is the fix.

      Which way a panel scans depends on how the glass is bonded and how
      the vendor configured MADCTL, so it differs per board and cannot be
      inferred from another one.

      CrowPanel 1.28in: VERIFIED on hardware -- text was mirrored without
      this, so it defaults to y.

      This affects the DISPLAY ONLY. The touch controller has its own
      independent options below -- see the note there, because the obvious
      assumption is wrong.

config CAPSTAN_LCD_MIRROR_Y
    bool "Mirror the display vertically"
    default n
    help
      Flips top-to-bottom. Combined with MIRROR_X this gives a 180-degree
      rotation, which is what you want if the panel is simply mounted
      upside down.

config CAPSTAN_LCD_SWAP_XY
    bool "Swap the X and Y axes"
    default n
    help
      Transposes the display. Only meaningful on a panel whose native scan
      direction is portrait against a landscape frame -- unlikely on these
      square, round displays, but the knob exists because the alternative
      when you need it is editing the driver.

config CAPSTAN_LCD_SWAP_RB
    bool "Swap the red and blue channels"
    depends on CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default n
    help
      The MaTouch 2.1in colour-channel order cannot be settled from the
      vendor documentation: the vendor's own firmware comments label GPIO
      11/15/12/16/21 as R0..R4, while the vendor's own wiki table labels the
      same pins B0..B4. They are the same physical wires -- the Arduino
      driver reconciles it by passing a BGR flag -- so one of the two labels
      is simply wrong, and which one can only be settled by looking at the
      panel.

      The pin map follows the wiki convention. If the boot test screen shows
      its red and blue bars swapped, enable this and rebuild. That is a
      one-line change here rather than an edit to the pin map, which should
      keep matching the schematic.

comment "Addressable RGB LED ring"

config CAPSTAN_HAS_RGB_LEDS
    bool
    default y if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    default y if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default n if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    help
      Whether the selected board physically has a ring of addressable
      (WS2812) LEDs around the display.

      This has no prompt on purpose -- it is a property of the hardware, not
      a preference, so it is derived from the board choice and cannot be set
      to something the board cannot do. Use CAPSTAN_RGB_LEDS_ENABLED to turn
      the feature off on a board that has it.

        Elecrow CrowPanel 1.28in   5 LEDs on GPIO48
        Elecrow CrowPanel 1.46in   8 LEDs on GPIO48, power enable on GPIO17
        Makerfabs MaTouch 2.1in    none fitted

config CAPSTAN_RGB_LED_COUNT
    int
    default 5 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    default 8 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default 0
    help
      Number of LEDs in the ring. Derived from the board; mirrored from
      BOARD_WS2812_COUNT in board_pins.h, and a compile-time assertion keeps
      the two from drifting apart.

config CAPSTAN_RGB_LEDS_ENABLED
    bool "Use the RGB LED ring"
    depends on CAPSTAN_HAS_RGB_LEDS
    default y
    help
      Drive the LED ring. Turn this off to leave it dark on a board that has
      one -- useful for a dark sleeping area, for measuring idle current, or
      while debugging something the LEDs would distract from.

      The ring is intended to carry state the user can read across a room
      without looking directly at the screen: the climate call colour while
      heating or cooling, an alert pulse, a confirmation flash. Per-screen
      behaviour is not implemented yet -- this setting and the capability
      flag exist so screens can be written against them.

config CAPSTAN_RGB_LEDS_MAX_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Maximum LED ring brightness (percent)"
    depends on CAPSTAN_RGB_LEDS_ENABLED
    range 1 100
    default 40
    help
      Ceiling applied to every LED write. These rings are bright enough at
      full scale to be unpleasant on a wall at night, and the 1.46in board
      fits eight of them. 40 percent is a reasonable default for an indoor
      wall panel.

comment "Touch"

config CAPSTAN_TOUCH_ENABLED
    bool "Enable the touch controller"
    default n
    help
      Off by default: the ring drives every screen, and nothing in the UI is
      touchable (there is no Back chip -- a long press goes back). With this
      off the touch controller and its I2C bus are never started, which
      saves a few KB of RAM, a little boot time, and on the CrowPanels the
      interrupt that woke the LVGL task on every press of the glass.

      Turn it on for a screen that needs touch, and for the bring-up
      build (no EEZ export), whose calibration and test screens are touch
      screens.

config CAPSTAN_TOUCH_MIRROR_X
    bool "Mirror touch horizontally"
    depends on CAPSTAN_TOUCH_ENABLED
    default n
    help
      INDEPENDENT of the display mirror, and usually NOT the same value.

      The intuition is that display and touch must carry the same
      transform. That is wrong, and testing showed why: the display mirror
      changes how the framebuffer maps onto the glass, while the touch
      controller is a separate device whose coordinates are fixed by how
      ITS layer is bonded. On the CrowPanel 1.28in the touch layer already
      reports in the corrected orientation, so the display needs
      MIRROR_X=y and touch needs MIRROR_X=n. Setting both mirrored the
      touch back to front while the display looked perfect.

      Work them out separately on each board. Correct display + wrong
      touch is the giveaway that these two were tied together.

config CAPSTAN_TOUCH_MIRROR_Y
    bool "Mirror touch vertically"
    depends on CAPSTAN_TOUCH_ENABLED
    default n

config CAPSTAN_TOUCH_SWAP_XY
    bool "Swap touch X and Y axes"
    depends on CAPSTAN_TOUCH_ENABLED
    default n

comment "Input"

config CAPSTAN_ENCODER_STEPS_PER_DETENT
    int "Quadrature counts per detent"
    range 1 8
    default 4 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_128
    default 2 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_CROWPANEL_146
    default 4 if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    help
      How many counts the full 4x quadrature decoder produces for one
      mechanical detent. The UI advances one item per detent, so this is the
      divisor.

      Too LOW and the ring is hypersensitive: one detent the user feels moves
      the selection by two, and reaching a specific item means turning by
      fractions of a click. Too HIGH and detents are swallowed. Either way the
      giveaway is that the count of items moved is a fixed multiple of the
      count of detents felt.

      CrowPanel 1.28in: VERIFIED on hardware -- 4. It was 2, taken from the
      EC3501 C15H30P3 datasheet's "1 pulse per 2 detents", but section 4-2 of
      that same datasheet contradicts it with 30 pulses per revolution per
      phase, which gives 4. The hardware agrees with section 4-2: at 2 every
      detent moved the selection by two. Trust the board, not the part number.

      CrowPanel 1.46in: VERIFIED on hardware -- 2. An earlier change raised
      this to 4 while trying to fix the 1.28in, and at 4 it took two detents
      to move the selection once. The two boards are pin-identical but do NOT
      share an encoder, and they do not share this value either -- 1.28in is
      4, 1.46in is 2. Do not re-derive either from the other.

      MaTouch 2.1in: the encoder part is not documented either; 4 is the
      common case for panel-mount encoders.

      Turn on CAPSTAN_ENCODER_DEBUG, turn the ring one detent, and read the
      count -- that is the only authority for a board not marked VERIFIED
      above.

config CAPSTAN_ENCODER_INVERT
    bool "Invert rotation direction"
    default y if CAPSTAN_BOARD_MATOUCH_21
    default n
    help
      Clockwise must increase, counter-clockwise must decrease. That is what
      people expect, and getting it backwards makes the whole device feel
      wrong even though nothing is technically broken.

      Which way the raw quadrature counts depends on how the encoder is
      mounted, so it differs between boards and can differ between
      production runs of the same board. It has to be checked on hardware.

      MaTouch 2.1in: VERIFIED on hardware -- counts backwards without this,
      so it defaults to y.

      CrowPanel 1.28in / 1.46in: NOT yet verified. Both default to n, which
      is a guess. Check with the bring-up test screen and correct it here.

config CAPSTAN_ENCODER_DEBUG
    bool "Log raw encoder steps"
    default n
    help
      Logs every raw quadrature step and button edge. Use once, to calibrate
      CAPSTAN_ENCODER_STEPS_PER_DETENT, then turn it off -- it is noisy
      enough to affect frame rate while the ring is turning.

config CAPSTAN_TOUCH_DEFAULT_ENABLED
    bool "Enable the touchscreen at boot"
    default n
    help
      Leave this OFF for normal builds.

      The ring is the primary input -- rotate to select, press to confirm,
      long press to go back -- and on these units the whole panel is the
      button, so pressing the ring IS pressing the screen. With touch live,
      one physical press produces both an encoder ENTER and a touch event,
      and they fight: the press lands on whatever is under the user's finger
      instead of on whatever the ring had focused.

      Touch is therefore off by default and switched on only by the screens
      that genuinely need pointer input -- the on-screen keyboard, and any
      list where hitting a target directly beats rotating to it. Those
      screens call capstan_board_touch_set_enabled() on entry and again on
      exit.

      Turn this on only to test the touch controller itself.

config CAPSTAN_LONG_PRESS_MS
    int "Ring long-press duration (ms)"
    range 300 3000
    default 700
    help
      Held for at least this long, the ring press returns to the menu
      instead of confirming.

comment "Behaviour"

config CAPSTAN_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Seconds of inactivity before the idle clock appears"
    range 5 3600
    default 30

config CAPSTAN_ALARM_SNOOZE_MIN
    int "Minutes an acknowledged alarm stays quiet"
    range 1 240
    default 10
    help
      Initial value only. Pressing the overlay acknowledges every alarm on
      it; one that is still active after this long is raised again. The
      user changes it on the device under Settings > Alarm Snooze, and the
      value is stored in NVS.

config CAPSTAN_DEFAULT_UNITS_CELSIUS
    bool "Default to Celsius"
    default n
    help
      Initial value only. The units setting is stored in NVS and the user can
      change it on the device.

config CAPSTAN_DEFAULT_THEME_DARK
    bool "Default to the dark theme"
    default n

comment "Connectivity"

config CAPSTAN_MQTT_DEFAULT_PORT
    int "Default MQTT port"
    default 8883
    help
      The Headwaters Mosquitto instance listens only on 8883 with TLS and
      requires a username and password; there is no plaintext 1883 listener.
      This is the value the settings screen starts from -- the user can
      change it, and whatever they set is stored in NVS.

config CAPSTAN_MQTT_TLS_SKIP_VERIFY
    bool "Accept the broker's self-signed certificate"
    default y
    help
      Headwaters ships a self-signed certificate and no CA is provisioned on
      the device, so the certificate is not verified. Turn this off only once
      a CA is actually being flashed, or every connection will fail.

comment "Development"

config CAPSTAN_PANEL_SMOKE_TEST
    bool "Panel smoke test at boot"
    default n
    help
      Immediately after the panel is initialised -- and before LVGL draws
      anything -- fill the screen red, then green, then blue, for about a
      second each, writing straight through esp_lcd.

      This isolates the two halves of a black screen. If the bars appear,
      the panel, its pin map, its timings and its init sequence are all
      correct and the fault is above, in LVGL or the flush path. If the
      screen stays black through all three, the fault is in the panel layer
      and LVGL is irrelevant.

      It also identifies the colour channel order without involving LVGL at
      all, which is useful on the MaTouch where the vendor's own firmware
      and wiki disagree about it.

      Costs about a second of boot time. Leave it off for normal builds.

config CAPSTAN_PANEL_SMOKE_TEST_DWELL_MS
    int "Smoke test: milliseconds per colour"
    depends on CAPSTAN_PANEL_SMOKE_TEST
    range 200 10000
    default 3000

config CAPSTAN_PANEL_SMOKE_TEST_CYCLES
    int "Smoke test: number of R/G/B cycles"
    depends on CAPSTAN_PANEL_SMOKE_TEST
    range 1 20
    default 3
    help
      Total test time is cycles x 3 x dwell. The defaults give 27 seconds,
      which is long enough that nobody has to catch it on the first boot.

config CAPSTAN_MOCK_DATA
    bool "Use mock data instead of MQTT"
    default n
    help
      Drives every screen from a synthetic data source so the UI can be
      exercised on the bench with no rig and no broker. Each value moves on a
      different period, deliberately -- values that sweep in lockstep hide
      layout bugs such as a box too narrow for its widest string.

endmenu

config CAPSTAN_WIFI_TX_POWER_QDBM
    int "Wi-Fi maximum transmit power (quarter-dBm)"
    range 8 80
    default 80
    help
      esp_wifi_set_max_tx_power() takes quarter-dBm, so 80 is 20 dBm
      (the default and the maximum) and 40 is 10 dBm.

      Lower this only to diagnose or work around a board whose supply
      cannot sustain transmit bursts. A radio that receives well but
      cannot be heard is the signature: transmit pulls roughly 300 mA in
      bursts while receive does not, so a marginal 3V3 rail breaks
      transmission first and leaves reception looking perfect.

      The MaTouch showed exactly that -- the best receive of the three
      boards (-13 dBm, 7 networks) while its soft AP never appeared in
      any scan and it could not associate with a network the other two
      could see.
